demo:cite_boehm1981_pas0 a aa:ConsultedPassage, owl:NamedIndividual ;
  rdfs:label "Passage 1 of boehm1981"@en ;
  aa:pageAnchor "l.1-11" ;
  aa:verbatimText "Software defects discovered after deployment cost dramatically more to correct than defects caught early in the lifecycle. Across empirical studies the cost ratio between a requirements-phase defect and a production-phase defect has been observed to range from approximately ten to one hundred, with the upper bound being most consistent in large systems where downstream rework involves re-running integration and acceptance tests across multiple subsystems. The economic implication for management is that prevention investment, measured as a fraction of total project cost, is recovered many times" .
